# UI/UX first — default scope for Cursor

**Default assumption:** prioritize **user-facing design and behavior** over **backend / pipeline implementation**.

## Prefer (when the task is open-ended or ambiguous)

- **`app/`** screens and navigation flow  
- **`src/components/`** layout, composition, interaction, loading/empty states  
- **`src/styles/`** Theme, motion, typography, spacing, accessibility  
- Viewfinder / overlay / results **presentation** (including wiring props and local UI state)  
- Copy, hierarchy, motion, and **Research Palette** usage  

## Defer or minimize unless explicitly requested

- New or heavy changes in **`src/services/`** (Gemini, OCR, classifier, analysis orchestration)  
- API keys, env, or server-style logic  
- Expanding **`src/data/`** beyond what the UI needs to render  
- Deep pipeline refactors (“wire the whole stack end-to-end”) when the ask was visual or experiential  

## How this works with `CLAUDE.md`

- **`CLAUDE.md`** remains the **product and stack reference** (vision, palette rules, `Ingredient` shape, pipeline map, commands). **Do not contradict** it on constraints (e.g. no red/green traffic lights, motion from `motion.ts`).  
- **`CLAUDE.md`** is written for **full-stack context** (e.g. Claude Code). In **this Cursor workspace**, use it to **stay correct**, but **choose UI/UX as the primary deliverable** when the user does not specify backend work.  
- If the user wants backend work, they will say so (e.g. “change `geminiService`”, “add allergen rule”). Then services are in scope.

## When unsure

Prefer **mocked data, placeholders, or existing service outputs** to ship UI, and **ask one short question** before expanding backend scope.
